      "card_ids": ["C01","C06","C07","C11"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Chlorophenyl triazolobenzodiazepine analogs show human oral F values of 44-100%, all above the 20% threshold; triazolam also shows high Caco-2 permeability (16.5 ×10^-6 cm/s), supporting the absorption component.",
      "card_ids": ["C05","C16","C17","C20","C23","C25","C28","C10","C12","C13","C18","C33","C40","C43","C62"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Trazodone, a chlorophenyl heteroaromatic lactam with a basic amine, has absolute oral F of 65-90% in humans and high F in dogs.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C21","C24","C27","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 permeability records for lipophilic heteroaromatic drugs (midazolam, triazolam, etoricoxib, DMP, and other compounds) show high Caco-2 permeability; no query-specific efflux or intestinal-metabolism record affirmatively establishes F <20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C58","C61","C62","C65","C71","C73","C78","C79"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only low F values are rat-specific (alprazolam 4.5%, fasudil 9.46%, baclofen metabolites very low) and are contradicted by human absolute F values in the same contexts; loperamide P-gp efflux is not query-specific.",
      "card_ids": ["C30","C42","C32","C05","C20","C28","C08","C75"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77","C78","C79","C80","C81"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct query-specific absolute oral bioavailability measurement exists.",
    "No query-specific fed-state pharmacokinetic or food-effect data are available.",
    "No query-specific Caco-2 permeability, efflux, or intestinal-metabolism measurement exists; L5 records are for other molecules.",
    "P-gp substrate status of the query is unknown; the loperamide P-gp record is not transferable without query-specific evidence.",
    "High logP/logD and low predicted solubility remain physicochemical concerns but are not affirmative evidence for F <20%.",
    "Structural transferability is qualitative; no numeric similarity score is supplied."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. L1/L2 human absolute F values for structurally related N-methyl quinolinone/heteroaromatic chlorophenyl drugs (tasquinimod, doravirine, triazolobenzodiazepines, trazodone) are all well above 20%; L3 exposure records are not threshold-relevant; L4/L5 permeability records support high absorption for lipophilic heteroaromatic compounds, while no query-specific efflux or intestinal-metabolism evidence affirmatively establishes F <20%. Fed state is not expected to negate the high prediction based on available analog food-effect data."
}

